In article <CLxrv1.1xA@freenet.carleton.ca>, aj883@FreeNet.Carleton.CA (Bruce
Clement) says:
>
>First ... the dumb death.  Walking, nay strolling along happily with 400+
>hit points.  Nothing has been able to even bug me for a while...just polished
>off a treasure zoo and my satiated stat disappears.  Two steps later....
>You see here an egg. Yum!  Eat said egg.  Two more steps and ....
>Your limbs are stiffening!
>You turn to stone!
>You are a statue!
>You die...

If I'm not mistaken, most cockatrice eggs are left behind by real
cockatrices.  Was there one on the level?  Aside from that, it's
just a chance you take.  You should've had enough time to fix yourself
when you saw "Your limbs are stiffening".  You get the antidote much
sooner than you meet any cockatrices (or their eggs).

Although ti feels stupid, this sort of death is eminently avoidable:
-- Don't eat random eggs if you're really not hungry.
-- If you do eat an egg, don't be stressed or strained so much that
   you don't have time to pull out the proper antidote.

>Bad idea number one on the same vein.  I was polymorphed into a red dragon.
>No problem...I like breathing fire and all.  But wait ... what's this
>#sit command do?  You lay an egg!  Alright! Instant food!  Can you say
>BoH full of eggs?

Sorry.  The Bad Idea award goes to you, not the Nethack Dev Team.
Take another look at that.  "You lay an egg!  Instant food!"
Do YOU think it's a good idea to eat your children?  The Nethack Gods
and the DEV Team sure don't!

> Roughly 40 #sit's later I had a nice ton of eggs and
>took off.  A little later .. your pack moves.  A baby red dragon jumps out
>and say's Poppa!  Real cute.  You guessed it.  Before long I am surronded
>by roughly 40 baby red dragons.  Sound good?  Wrong.

Again.  Why do monsters lay eggs?  To eat their children?  Wrong.
It's to have nice little baby critters run around and do things.
And then the babies learn to kill and eat and grow and kill and eat
and grow.  Before you know it, your pet baby red dragons are pet
full-grown red dragons.  Wouldn't You like to have a few pet dragons
following you around?

>1.  You can't move anywhere.

If you can't displace them, you can always dig around them.
Oh, you Don't keep at least one wand of digging around?

>2.  Anyone of them can kill you first.

Not if they're tame.  Of course, they might get confused from hunger,
in which case you leave and come back when they've grown wild.

>3.  Any monster that comes near is put in the blender and eaten.

That is the way of pets.  Some people like them, some don't.

>4.  You don't die from attacks..you starve.
>
>Really sucks!

It all comes down to how you look at a neat feature of the game.
Next time when a new thought comes to you, make sure to examine
it from the side of "would this be considered lawful or moral".
I think the DEV team has done admirably in their efforts to have
alignment and devotion make sense in this game.  Don't ignore it.

Roger Christman


